// Four-bar singular worked example
//
// Demonstrates the kinematic-singularity diagnostic (task 3580 / KCC-alpha,
// GR-039): a closed four-body loop whose loop-closure Jacobian is
// rank-deficient at the modeled configuration.
//
// Four-bar framing: four bodies (solidA, solidB, solidC, solidD) form ONE
// closed loop, closed by a single redundant edge.  solidA hangs directly off
// world via j_x (the spanning-tree edge for what will become the closing
// joint); solidB hangs directly off world via j_a; solidC stacks j_b on top
// of solidB's frame, giving the closing side a TWO-DEEP walk; solidD
// re-anchors the SAME j_x onto j_b, solidC's own `at` (closing edge) — this
// is the loop's closing joint pair: j_x / j_b.
//
// Free variables come from the CLOSING side only: extract_loop_closure_chains
// resolves every joint on chain_a and iterates none of them.  The record here
// is path_a = [world, j_x] against path_b = [world, j_a, j_b], so binding j_x
// leaves j_a and j_b as the two free variables, and both act along the SAME
// +X axis, so their finite-difference Jacobian columns are identical → rank-1
// JᵀJ → NewtonOutcome::Singular.
//
// INVARIANT this fixture pins: the two identical Jacobian columns come from
// TWO genuinely distinct collinear +X prismatics on the closing side (j_a and
// j_b — a two-deep walk), never from one joint composed twice.  A closing
// joint present on both recorded paths would manufacture a false rank
// deficiency, so keep the closing side two joints deep if this fixture is ever
// re-homed.
//
// j_x/j_a/j_b use three DIFFERENT ranges (rather than three identical
// prismatic(vec3(1,0,0), 0mm .. 1000mm) calls) so that:
//   1. Their Values are structurally distinct — identical-range joints
//      would be byte-identical Value::Maps that alias in joint_parents,
//      collapsing the intended 2-free-joint/1-loop-closure topology into a
//      spurious second closure.
//   2. The Newton closure residual is non-zero at the starting guess — the
//      free side starts at midpoint(j_a) + midpoint(j_b) = 1050mm + 1250mm
//      = 2300mm, which never collides with j_x's range (0..1000mm), so
//      Newton must actually invert the rank-deficient Jacobian rather than
//      converging trivially at iteration 0.
//
// Topology mirrors
// crates/reify-eval/tests/harness_fea_solver_e2e/kinematic_singularity_snapshot_e2e.rs::SINGULAR_SOURCE
// (task 3580's proven end-to-end fixture for this exact solver code path).
//
// MAINTENANCE: this body/joint wiring is a deliberate duplicate of
// SINGULAR_SOURCE (committed example vs. inline test fixture — see rationale
// above). If the solver's singularity conditions ever change in a way that
// requires editing one topology, update the other in the SAME change so
// neither fixture silently drifts into testing a stale/no-longer-singular
// chain; both tests assert is_singular=true and will fail loudly if you miss
// one, but keeping them in lockstep is currently a manual step.
//
// Considered and deferred: a shared const or a test that diffs this file's
// structure body against SINGULAR_SOURCE byte-for-byte, so drift is caught
// structurally instead of only via each fixture's own is_singular assertion.
// Not done here because SINGULAR_SOURCE is a private const inside
// kinematic_singularity_snapshot_e2e.rs, a separate `tests/` integration
// binary — exporting it (e.g. via reify-test-support) is a cross-cutting
// test-infra change outside this task's file scope, not a same-file fix.
//
// PRD ref: docs/prds/v0_3/kinematic-constraints-completion.md (KCC-iota, task 3848)
structure def FourBarSingular {
    let j_x = prismatic(vec3(1, 0, 0), 0mm .. 1000mm)
    let j_a = prismatic(vec3(1, 0, 0), 0mm .. 2100mm)
    let j_b = prismatic(vec3(1, 0, 0), 0mm .. 2500mm)

    let m0 = mechanism()
    let m1 = body(m0, "solidA", j_x)
    let m2 = body(m1, "solidB", j_a)
    let m3 = body(m2, "solidC", j_b, j_a)
    let m4 = body(m3, "solidD", j_x, j_b)

    // Loop joint pair.  j_x is the CLOSING joint: it appears as `at` twice —
    // once on solidA (the spanning-tree edge straight to world) and again on
    // solidD (the closing edge, re-anchoring it onto j_b) — and is composed
    // exactly once, at chain_a's tail.  j_b is the closing edge's PARENT and
    // chain_b's terminal; it is solidC's own `at`, parented to j_a, so it
    // rides j_a's frame.  j_x is the bound driver; j_a and j_b are the two
    // free, collinear +X variables the closing side hands to Newton.
    let snap = snapshot(m4, [bind(j_x, 500mm)])
}
